<SiteLayout title="How it works" description="How HonorCart detects referrals, stands down, and tests coupons safely."><PageHero eyebrow="How it works" title="Savings without the switch." lead="HonorCart separates coupon help from affiliate attribution. The extension checks the referral state first, then decides what it is allowed to do."/><section class="section shell"><div class="step-list"><article><b>01</b><div><p class="eyebrow">Arrival</p><h2>HonorCart checks for a referral signal.</h2><p>On supported merchants, the extension looks for recognized network parameters and creator identifiers. It minimizes collection and does not need a shopper’s identity to make the stand-down decision.</p></div></article><article><b>02</b><div><p class="eyebrow">Decision</p><h2>Protection is decided before monetization.</h2><p>If an eligible existing referral is recognized, that merchant session is marked protected. Server-controlled rules disable HonorCart monetization for the session.</p></div></article><article><b>03</b><div><p class="eyebrow">Savings</p><h2>Coupons can still be tested where safe.</h2><p>HonorCart may test public coupon codes without activating its own affiliate relationship. If safe testing cannot be supported, the extension stands down entirely.</p></div></article><article><b>04</b><div><p class="eyebrow">Proof</p><h2>Creators can see protection events.</h2><p>A privacy-minimized event records that a registered identifier was recognized and protected. Checkout value appears only where reliably observable and is always distinguished from a confirmed sale.</p></div></article></div></section><section class="callout shell"><h2>When there is no existing referral</h2><p>HonorCart may present an affiliate-backed offer, with a clear disclosure before activation. Merchant and network rules can disable monetization instantly.</p><a href="/affiliate-disclosure">Read the affiliate disclosure →</a></section></SiteLayout>
